{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Prior distribution of animal mass is positive semi-axis with upper bound of 30 tons (blue whale mass)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:47:40\u201300:48:13] 'First, they start from prior distribution of possible masses of animals on our planet. So this distribution has restricted support. So of course we understand that mass cannot be negative. So it's a positive semi-axis. And it has some form where, again, there's a theta max because no animal may have mass more than 30 tons. That is the mass of the blue whale.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "First wisdomer uses tactile observations to model $P(x | \\theta)$ where $x$ is tactile feeling and $\\theta$ is animal mass",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:48:44\u201300:49:16] 'this is kind of why with the function. So it shows that if an animal has a mass of theta, how probable is that I feel, what I feel, that I will measure x.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "After first observation (touching elephant's nose), posterior distribution $P(\\theta | x)$ suggests either a very large animal or a snake-like animal",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:49:51\u201300:50:27] 'So this is either nose of some very large animal, or this is a body of some snake-like animal.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Second wisdomer uses acoustic observations to model $P(y | \\theta)$ where $y$ is sound and $\\theta$ is animal mass",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:50:56\u201300:51:26] 'So he has completely different probabilistic model. So Q, Y, given theta. So Y is completely different variable. It lives in completely different dimension.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian update combines posterior from previous step as new prior with new likelihood to form new posterior",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:06\u201300:52:43] 'He uses posterior distribution as a new prior distribution. He multiplies it by its own likelihood. He normalizes the result.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Sequential Bayesian updates converge to a sharp posterior distribution around a specific mass",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] 'we will probably end up with a posterior distribution that will be pretty sharp around some small area.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian framework allows combining different probabilistic models into a single coherent estimate",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] 'This is one of the advantages of Bayesian framework. We can combine different probabilistic models and we can build a more complicated model from a simple ones like a wall from building blocks.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The process can be repeated many times to incorporate multiple observations",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] 'And we can do this many, many, many times until we get something that will be conditioned on a large number of observations.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR mentions 'elephant' but the example is about a general animal (with the elephant being a specific example). The notes correctly identify this as an example but the ASR doesn't explicitly say 'elephant' in the context of the final estimate."
  }
